  "educational": {
    "scientificImportance": "Peppermint is a sterile interspecific hybrid (M. aquatica × M. spicata) propagated entirely by rhizome, making it a useful model for understanding secondary metabolite biosynthesis in glandular trichomes and the terpenoid pathway from IPP through the MEP/DXP route to menthol.",
    "studentRelevance": "Its extremely high secondary metabolite output (~0.9 baseline) relative to every other plant in this simulator gives students a direct comparison case for how terpene investment trades off against growth rate and stress tolerance.",
    "indoorOutdoor": "both",
    "realWorldIssue": "Heavy-metal uptake into mint leaves used as culinary and medicinal herbs poses a direct food-safety risk in urban gardens with contaminated soils, with cadmium and lead concentrations in leaf tissue exceeding regulatory limits near industrial sites."
  },
